{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"648\t/**\n649\t * Capture any *other* session in this project whose transcript has grown\n650\t * since it was last read.\n651\t *\n652\t * This exists because a session's own last lines are unreachable to it. The\n653\t * hook fires, capture reads to end-of-file, and Claude Code then writes more\n654\t * — the closing bookkeeping of the final turn, and crucially the session's\n655\t * last `file-history-snapshot`, which is the most complete one it will ever\n656\t * write. No further hook fires for that session, so those lines sat stranded\n657\t * behind a cursor nothing would ever advance again. Observed live: a cursor\n658\t * resting at line 40 of a 50-line transcript, with the only snapshot naming a\n659\t * real backup at line 49.\n660\t *\n661\t * The next session in the same project closes the gap. Staleness is decided\n662\t * by file size against the size recorded with the cursor, so the sweep stats\n663\t * each transcript rather than parsing it, and reads only the ones that grew.\n664\t *\n665\t * A transcript with *no* cursor is skipped, and that bound is the whole point\n666\t * of the pass. A missing cursor does not mean \"this session has a gap\", it\n667\t * means cledger never captured this session at all — it predates the install,\n668\t * or the user did not want it. Adopting those would turn a gap-closing sweep\n669\t * into a silent backfill of every conversation ever held in the repo, and\n670\t * would anchor each one to whatever HEAD happens to be checked out now rather\n671\t * than to the commit it was actually about. A session cledger already tracks\n672\t * is the only session it has any business finishing.\n673\t *\n674\t * Failures are swallowed per session: a sweep is a bonus pass over\n675\t * conversations the user is not currently having, and must never be the\n676\t * reason their live capture reports an error.\n677\t 